  "g6-chemistry-c1-l1": {
    "id": "g6-chemistry-c1-l1",
    "chapterId": "g6-chemistry-c1",
    "title": "Objects, Materials, Lustre & Hardness",
    "desc": "Objects vs materials, classification criteria, physical appearance, metallic lustre, and hardness scratch testing.",
    "content": "# Objects, Materials, Lustre & Hardness\n\n## 1. Learning Objectives\n- Distinguish between an object and the materials from which it is made.\n- Understand the scientific need for grouping objects based on physical properties.\n- Differentiate between lustrous (shiny) and non-lustrous materials.\n- Perform a scratch test to classify materials as hard or soft.\n\n## 2. Introduction\nLook around your study room. You see a wooden desk, a plastic ruler, an iron key, a glass tumbler, and a paper notebook. All these items are **objects**. Each object is made of one or more **materials**. Grouping materials based on their physical properties helps us study their characteristics systematically and choose the right material for making specific items.\n\n## 3. Detailed Concept Explanation\n### Objects vs. Materials\n- **Object:** Anything that can be seen or touched and has a specific shape and purpose (e.g., chair, spoon, bottle).\n- **Material:** The matter or substance from which an object is made (e.g., wood, plastic, iron, cotton, glass).\n- *Observation:* A single object can be made from a combination of materials (e.g., a knife has a steel blade and a plastic handle), and a single material can be used to make many different objects (e.g., wood can be used to make chairs, tables, doors, and bullock carts).\n\n### Why Do We Group Materials?\nGrouping materials into categories based on similarities and differences is called **classification**.\n- **Convenience:** Makes storing and locating items easy (e.g., a grocer keeps all soaps in one corner and biscuits in another).\n- **Systematic Study:** Helps scientists study patterns in physical and chemical properties.\n\n### Property 1: Appearance and Metallic Lustre\nMaterials differ in their visual appearance, surface texture, and shine.\n- **Lustrous Materials:** Materials that have a shiny surface when freshly cut or polished.\n  - *Examples:* Metals like Copper, Aluminum, Gold, Silver, Iron.\n  - *Note:* Some metals lose their shine and appear dull when exposed to moist air for a long time due to chemical reactions. Freshly cutting or rubbing them with sandpaper reveals their shiny lustre.\n- **Non-Lustrous Materials:** Materials that have a dull surface appearance and do not shine.\n  - *Examples:* Wood, Paper, Cardboard, Chalk, Cotton, Rubber.\n\n### Property 2: Hardness vs. Softness\nWe test hardness by attempting to compress or scratch a material with an iron nail or key.\n- **Soft Materials:** Materials that can be easily compressed or scratched.\n  - *Examples:* Sponge, Cotton wool, Candle wax, Chalk, Soap, Butter.\n- **Hard Materials:** Materials that are difficult to compress or scratch.\n  - *Examples:* Iron nail, Granite stone, Diamond, Glass sheet, Wood block.\n\nThe study of **Objects, Materials, Lustre & Hardness** provides fundamental insights into matter, chemical properties, and physical transformation techniques. When conducting chemistry experiments or solving conceptual problems, always follow step-by-step scientific methods.\n\n### Systematic Chemical Study Approach\n1. Observe physical properties, state changes, and mixing behavior.\n2. Select appropriate classification or separation methods based on physical characteristics.\n3. Execute experimental procedures carefully.\n4. Record observations and draw verified conclusions.\n\n## 4. Important Definitions\n- **Material:**  The matter or substance used to manufacture objects (e.g. wood, steel, glass).\n- **Lustre:**  The natural shiny appearance of a freshly cut or polished metal surface.\n- **Classification:**  The systematic process of grouping objects based on shared similarities and differences.\n\n## 5. Key Concepts\n- Materials are classified based on physical properties like lustre, hardness, solubility, density, and transparency.\n- Separation methods rely on differences in physical properties (size, weight, state, solubility) of components.\n- Temperature changes alter solubility limits and phase transformations.\n\n## 6. Examples\n### Example 1: Classify: Iron key, Wooden block, Cotton ball, Gold ring\n*Solution:* Iron key = Lustrous & Hard. Wooden block = Non-lustrous & Hard. Cotton ball = Non-lustrous & Soft. Gold ring = Lustrous & Hard.\n\n### Example 2: Why is a tumbler not made with a piece of cloth?\n*Solution:* Cloth has porous holes and cannot hold liquids. A tumbler must be made of a non-porous material like glass, plastic, or metal.\n\n### Example 3: Why does an old copper vessel look dull?\n*Solution:* Copper reacts with moist air over time forming a dull green coating. Cleaning it with lemon or sandpaper exposes its lustrous metal surface.\n\n## 7. Step-by-Step Explanation\n### Experimental & Analytical Method\n1. Identify the given substances or mixture components.\n2. Determine key physical properties (particle size, density, solubility).\n3. Choose the appropriate chemical classification or separation technique.\n4. Perform operational procedures sequentially.\n5. Summarize final results and observations clearly.\n\n## 8. Formulas / Rules\n- **Filtration Rule:**\n  $$\\text{Total Mixture} = \\text{Filtrate (Liquid)} + \\text{Residue (Solid trapped on Filter)}$$\n- **Evaporation Recovery:**\n  $$\\text{Solute Recovery} = \\text{Evaporate Liquid Solvent} \\rightarrow \\text{Solid Solute Remains}$$\n- **Solubility Rule:**\n  $$\\text{Solubility of Solute} \\propto \\text{Temperature of Solution}$$\n\n## 9. Worked Problems / Activities\n### Worked Problem 1\nExplain with examples how a single material can be used to make multiple different objects.\n*Solution:* Plastic is a single material used to manufacture a wide variety of objects including buckets, water bottles, chairs, toys, ruler scales, and pen casings.\n\n### Worked Problem 2\nDescribe a simple scratch test activity to compare the hardness of a piece of wax and an iron nail.\n*Solution:* Take an iron key and attempt to scratch the surface of both items. The key easily cuts and scratches the wax (Soft Material), but cannot scratch the iron nail (Hard Material).\n\n## 10. Try Yourself\n1. Name 3 objects made from wood and 3 objects made from plastic.\n2. Why are handles of cooking pans made of plastic or wood instead of iron?\n\n## 11. Common Mistakes\n- Confusing an object (e.g. chair) with its material (e.g. wood).\n- Assuming tarnished metals are non-lustrous without testing a freshly cut edge.\n\n## 12. Quick Revision & Key Takeaways\n- Objects are made from one or more materials.\n- Grouping materials enables systematic study.\n- Lustrous = Shiny surface (metals).\n- Soft = Easily compressed/scratched; Hard = Difficult to compress/scratch."
  },
